}

Rules:
- Map tracker names to real products: "BD products - Proxy" -> Residential/DC/ISP Proxy & Web Unlocker; "BD products - Web Data" -> Web Scraper API & Datasets/Marketplace; "AI Terms" or "AI terms for Growth" -> LLM / AI scraping; "Video data" -> YouTube / video datasets.
- mostWantedProducts: TOP 3, ranked by how many meetings mention them. dealCount = number of meetings mentioning that product. evidence = short reason citing trackers or account names.
- useCasesAndPainPoints: 3 to 5 real themes from the briefs and keyPoints (examples: anti-bot / geo blocking on retail, large-scale AI training data, competitor switching, pricing / budget friction, compliance / legal, LinkedIn / lead enrichment). Cite only accounts that appear in the data.
- themeInsights: write ONE analytical paragraph of 2 to 3 sentences (MAXIMUM 260 characters) for EACH of these four fixed themes, in this exact order: "Pain Points", "Product & Pricing Clarity", "Objections & Hesitations", "Competitive Landscape". Each paragraph must describe how that theme showed up in THIS region's actual calls, citing real account names. For "Competitive Landscape", NAME the specific competitors mentioned. Do NOT include any numbers, percentages, or call counts in the insight text — those are added automatically afterward. If a theme barely appears, say so briefly. Keep all four themes even if short.
- notable: 2 to 3 highest-signal deals (late stage, large enterprise, or unusual scale) with a one-line reason each.
- competitorsMentioned: list EVERY competing data/proxy/scraper/data-enrichment vendor NAMED in the meetings — for example Oxylabs, NetNut, Apify, Zyte, Smartproxy, Decodo, Outscraper, Browserbase, Apollo, Lusha, ZoomInfo, or ANY other vendor a customer mentions, INCLUDING ones not in this list. NEVER include "Bright Data" itself. "name" = the competitor's name exactly as mentioned; "context" = one short phrase on how it came up; "accounts" = which accounts mentioned it. Return an empty array [] if no competitor is named.
- winLossByCompetitor: ONLY include an entry if the call brief or keyPoints EXPLICITLY state a reason Bright Data is being chosen over, or lost to, a named competitor. 
  Do NOT infer, guess, or generalize. Do NOT create an entry just because a competitor was named. 
  DO NOT create an entry for: data sources or feeds (e.g. "X/Twitter Firehose"), the customer's own legacy/in-house tools, tools the customer already abandoned before Bright Data (e.g. "Bionx"), unnamed competitors, generic "cheaper vendors", or website/anti-bot systems (e.g. "Ticketmaster", "Akamai"). Those are NOT competitors for this table. 
  "competitor" = vendor name exactly as written in the call. 
  "outcome" = "Win" only if the text says we are preferred/chosen; "Loss" only if the text says the customer leaned to the competitor; "Mixed" only if the text explicitly states both. 
  "reason" = copy the concrete reason as stated in the call, condensed (e.g. "better success rate on retail unblocking", "competitor cheaper for low volume"). 
  "quote" = a SHORT verbatim snippet from the brief/keyPoints that supports it (max ~120 chars). If you cannot provide a real supporting snippet, DO NOT include the entry. 
  "accounts" = the accounts where it was stated. 
  If NO call explicitly states a win/loss reason, return an empty array []. An empty array is the correct and expected answer when evidence is absent.
- summary: 2 to 3 sentences summarizing the region for a sales leader.
- Base every claim strictly on the provided data; never invent accounts, vendors, or facts. Some briefs are in Hebrew, French, Spanish or Portuguese — read them and always write output in English.

